After the model is built, before `boot()` finishes:
```js
TANK.root.updateMatrixWorld(true);
// (a) is the boot camera on the muzzle side?
const mz = _v1.setFromMatrixPosition(GUN.muzzleNode.matrixWorld).z;      // any mesh at the tube tip
console.assert(Math.sign(mz) === Math.sign(CAM.position.z), 'HERO FRAME = ENGINE DECK');
// (b) does W drive toward the nose? forward direction must have the same sign as mz
run driveUpdate(1.0) with throttle=+1, then check sign(TANK.pos.z - z0) === sign(mz);
// (c) is the sun ever degenerate, and does the shadow frustum contain the tank?
console.assert(SUNOFF.length() > 40, 'SUNOFF zero -> light == target');
// expected linear luminance before ACES, for choosing exposure/threshold:
// L = I_sun*max(NdotL,0)*albedoLinear/PI + I_hemi*albedoLinear/PI*0.9 + envDiffuse
// with I_sun 2.8, sand albedoLinear(0x9d8f76) ~ (0.335,0.275,0.181) -> L ~ 0.24-0.40
```
That arithmetic is why you must **not** change `toneMappingExposure = 1.02` or the sun intensity blind: 0.38 linear through ACES(1.02) is already ~sRGB 0.62 sand.

--- 1. Single size source of truth (fixes soft first frame on every Retina/HiDPI display, and the fake "2x" PNG) ---
`onResize` (read 2366-2375) calls `RENDER.setSize` then `composer.setSize(w,h)` and then **re-sizes bloom and GTAO with CSS pixels**, overwriting the drawing-buffer size the composer just propagated. On dpr=2 that computes bloom mips and AO depth/normal targets at half the render resolution (thick AO halos around the tracks, blocky glow). Also `savePNG` (2351-2364) and `applyQuality` (2161) change `renderer.setPixelRatio`, which resizes the canvas but never the composer's cached ratio.
```js
function syncSizes() {
  const w = innerWidth, h = innerHeight, pr = RENDER.getPixelRatio();
  CAM.aspect = w / h; CAM.updateProjectionMatrix();
  RENDER.setSize(w, h, false);
  if (POST.composer) {
    if (POST.composer.setPixelRatio) POST.composer.setPixelRatio(pr);
    POST.composer.setSize(w, h);        // -> effective w*pr for EVERY pass
  }
  /* delete POST.bloom.setSize(w,h) and POST.ao.setSize(w,h): the composer owns them */
}
```
Use `onResize = syncSizes`; call `syncSizes()` after every `setPixelRatio` in `applyQuality` and in `savePNG` (before `render()`, and again after restoring). This matters automatically: AUTO quality changes the pixel ratio on its own ~1.2 s after boot.

--- 2. Photo-mode DOF: aperture is ~60x too strong ---
`BokehPass(..., { aperture: 0.03, maxblur: 0.012 })` (read 2124; E399 shows this was 0.00035/0.009 before an edit) with the per-frame driver `u.aperture = 0.006 + dofT*0.055` (read 2338). In BokehShader the CoC is `clamp((depth-focus)*aperture, ±maxblur)` in UV, so saturation happens at `maxblur/aperture` metres: 0.012/0.03 = **0.4 m** now, 0.012/0.006 = 2 m at the soft end. The tank is 8-10 m long and the sand recedes to the horizon, so nearly the whole photo frame sits at max blur. Set `aperture: 0.0012, maxblur: 0.011` and drive `focus` as now, `u.aperture.value = 0.0006 + CAMSTATE.dofT*0.0028` (saturation 4-20 m) — the subject stays tack-sharp, 10-20 m of sand softens, the horizon mush is real DOF instead of a smear.
Caution: `BokehPass` samples `nearClip/farClip` uniforms captured at construction. If you also raise the camera near plane (2400: `near 0.12` -> 0.25, free depth precision at the tank; pair with `OrbitControls.minDistance >= 5` so you cannot clip into the hull), update `POST.bokeh.materialBokeh.uniforms.nearClip/farClip` too, or the linearised depth — and therefore the focus distance — is wrong.

--- 3. One lighting definition only, and never let the sun degenerate ---
The read shows two sets: `buildEnvironment` (972-987: FogExp2 0xb9b3a0, sun 3.5, fill 0.42, hemi 0.42) and boot (2413-2423: FogExp2 again, `SUN = DirectionalLight(...,2.8)`, `HemisphereLight(...,0.45)`). Verify which executes; if both add to the same scene you get ~2x directional light (sand near the ACES shoulder, paint hue crushed). Keep the fallback, but make it the same numbers and always non-degenerate:
```js
if (SUNOFF.lengthSq() < 1600) SUNOFF.copy(sunVector(_v1).multiplyScalar(90));
SCENE.add(SUN); SCENE.add(SUN.target);            // target must be in the graph (982 does this; the fallback may not)
const d = SUNOFF.length(), c = SUN.shadow.camera, E = 13;   // +-13 m -> 12.7 mm/texel @2048
c.left=-E; c.right=E; c.top=E; c.bottom=-E; c.near=d*0.35; c.far=d*1.9; c.updateProjectionMatrix();
```
`SUN.position.set(TANK.pos + SUNOFF)` with `SUNOFF = (0,0,0)` (2377) makes light position == target: undefined direction, degenerate shadow matrix, possible black/NaN first frame on the fallback path. The `near=20/far=190` block (979) is only safe while `|SUNOFF| ~ 90`; deriving from `d` removes that coupling. Also add texel snapping in the loop after the sun is placed (2311-2315) — the comment says the sun follows the tank, and a continuously moving shadow frustum makes every shadow edge crawl while driving:
```js
const t = (2*E)/SUN.shadow.mapSize.x;
SUN.position.set(Math.round(SUN.position.x/t)*t, Math.round(SUN.position.y/t)*t, Math.round(SUN.position.z/t)*t);
```

--- 4. Material table (biggest pixel win per edit; targets, because the livery constants A.r/A.m are behind a table I did not read) ---
Real CARC paint is a dielectric; the current metalness on the biggest, most specular objects is the main "CG" tell.
- `MAT.gunSteel` (739) color 0x585d63, roughness 0.44, metalness 0.9, env 1.2 -> **roughness 0.62-0.68, metalness 0.55-0.65, env 0.9**. The barrel is a long cylinder crossing a third of the hero frame; at metalness 0.9/roughness 0.44 with only a 1024x512 PMREM sky it renders as one chrome streak. This is the single highest-leverage material change.
- `MAT.track` (736) 0.88/0.6 and `MAT.steel` (735) 0.92/0.55 -> **metalness 0.5-0.6 / 0.72-0.78, roughness 0.70 / 0.60-0.65**; warm the track albedo 0x6f7379 -> 0x5c584f (dust/oxide). High-metalness + low-detail env = uniform grey plastic-metal.
- `MAT.glass` (740) MeshPhysicalMaterial `metalness 0.55, roughness 0.05` -> **metalness 0.0, roughness 0.06, envMapIntensity 2.2-2.6**, keep clearcoat 1.0/0.04. Metallic glass makes every periscope/vision block a black mirror patch in the cupola silhouette.
- `MAT.armour` (732): force `metalness <= 0.05` (paint), `roughness 0.62-0.72` with the roughnessMap authored around 1.0 so the map, not the scalar, owns variation; `normalScale 0.55 -> 0.38-0.45` (0.55 on flat plates reads as orange peel).
- `MAT.lamp/lensRed/lensAmber` (741-743) emissive 0.4/0.5/0.35 can never cross the bloom threshold: raise to **1.4-2.4** so the lights actually glow, and raise bloom threshold to ~1.1 (see 5) so nothing else does.
- `MAT.mark` (749): white 0xffffff at metalness 0.05 is the brightest thing on the tank and reads as a sticker; use a weathered 0xd6d3c8 with roughness matching the paint, and make sure **both** `polygonOffsetFactor` and `polygonOffsetUnits` are negative (-3/-3).
- Leave alone: `MAT.rubber` (737, 0x1b1c1e/0.94/0.02) and `MAT.deck` (734, non-slip rough 1.0/metal 0.12) are physically correct as written, and `envMapIntensity 0.5-0.85` on the matte dielectrics is right — do not raise it.

--- 5. Bloom threshold in linear space; a conditional, not a blind edit ---
`UnrealBloomPass(res, 0.42, 0.62, 0.86)` (2119) is evaluated on the linear HDR image, before OutputPass/ACES. Sand sits ~0.24-0.40 linear, so the threshold is currently only catching metal speculars and the sky sun blob. If the first frame shows a milky veil around the sun and the white markings, move to `0.34, 0.55, 1.10`; then emissive parts must exceed 1.10 (item 4) to keep glowing. Do not lower the threshold to make lights glow.

--- 6. Hero framing: the muzzle, not the exhaust, must face the camera ---
Decisive layout facts: `idlerZ = -3.36`, `sprkZ = +3.36` (F0, real M1 has the drive sprocket at the rear), the upper hull deck (1.63) is flat toward +Z and breaks down over the last ~2 m toward -Z (rear engine deck), and the engine haze emitter uses `lz = +3.6` (E411, 2302-2307). All three say the rear is +Z, while boot puts the camera at `(8.4, 3.6, 10.5)` looking at (0,1.3,0) (read 2401-2402): distance 13.6 m, 9.7 deg elevation, 38.7 deg off the gun axis — i.e. a rear-right 3/4 with the gun pointing away. Mirror it and tighten the fill (currently the tank spans only ~27% of frame width):
```js
CAM.fov = 40; CAM.updateProjectionMatrix();
CAM.position.set(5.4, 2.6, -7.4);            // muzzle side; d=9.1 m, 8.5 deg elevation
CAM.lookAt(0, 1.25, -0.4);
CTRL.target.set(0, 1.25, -0.4); CTRL.minDistance = 5; CTRL.maxDistance = 45; CTRL.maxPolarAngle = 1.52;
```
Then assert (0a): the camera's z sign must equal the muzzle's. If assertion (0b) fails, `driveUpdate` moves the hull toward its own tail — with the tracks verified drift-free this is invisible to your existing Node checks and would be the most visible "unreal" defect in DRIVE mode. Optional garnish: give the turret a few degrees of initial yaw toward the camera so the tube crosses the frame diagonally instead of pointing away.

--- 7. Grounding and first-presented-frame warm-up (cheap, quality-independent) ---
